<body>

<a href="javascript:window.print()" class="pdf-btn">Save as PDF</a>

<!-- COVER PAGE -->
<div class="cover">
  <div class="bv-logo" aria-label="BuyerVoice.AI" style="display:inline-block;font-family:var(--serif);line-height:1;margin:0 auto 48px;">
  <span style="font-size:44px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:-0.03em;">BuyerVoice</span><span style="font-size:30px;font-weight:500;color:var(--signal);letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin-left:2px;">.AI</span>
</div>
  <h1>HR Leaders BGR:<br>Interview Research Report</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Understanding HR, Payroll, and Benefits Software<br>Evaluation and Purchase Decisions</p>
  <p class="meta">Prepared for BGR &bull; April 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; 180 participants &nbsp;|&nbsp; Qualitative interviews</p>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion" open>
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <span class="accordion-num">01</span>
    <span class="accordion-text">
      <span class="accordion-title">Executive Summary</span>
      <span class="accordion-subtitle">Research objectives, methodology, and key findings</span>
    </span>
    <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <h2>Research Objective</h2>
    <p>This study explored how HR, People Operations, and Talent Acquisition leaders at mid-market companies (50 to 1,000 employees) evaluate, select, and experience HR, payroll, and benefits software. The research captured the full purchase journey: from initial triggers and frustrations through evaluation criteria, competitive consideration, final selection rationale, and ongoing satisfaction with the chosen platform.</p>

    <h2>Methodology</h2>
    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">180</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Participants</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">24</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Questions</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">153</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Thematic Codes</div>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <div class="stat-num">2</div>
        <div class="stat-label">Segments (Exploratory)</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Participants were split evenly across three current platforms: BambooHR (60), Gusto (60), and Rippling (60). The sample spanned four company-size tiers from 50 to 1,000 employees, with representation across Technology, Healthcare, Professional Services, Financial Services, Retail, Education, and Manufacturing sectors.</p>

    <h2>Key Findings</h2>
    <div class="insight">
      <p><strong>69% cited manual process burden as the primary trigger for evaluation.</strong> Nearly seven in ten participants said that the weight of manual HR processes, such as data entry, approvals, and paper-based workflows, was the event that pushed them to start looking at new tools.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight">
      <p><strong>82% reported reporting inadequacy as a core frustration.</strong> The single most prevalent frustration across the entire study was the inability of current tools to produce the reports HR leaders need to inform business decisions and demonstrate strategic value.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight">
      <p><strong>Competitive pricing (54%) and intuitive UI (47%) drove selection decisions.</strong> When asked why they chose their current platform, more than half pointed to competitive pricing, while nearly half valued an intuitive user interface over feature depth.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight">
      <p><strong>82% rejected competitors for being overbuilt.</strong> The most common rejection reason was that competing platforms were too complex for the company's needs, reinforcing that mid-market buyers penalize unnecessary complexity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="insight">
      <p><strong>58% see a mid-market platform capability gap as an industry-wide challenge.</strong> More than half of participants said the biggest challenge in their space is that platforms are either built for small businesses or enterprises, leaving mid-market companies underserved.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Priority Recommendations</h2>
    <table class="rec-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Recommendation</th><th>Supporting Data</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1. Reporting</td><td>Invest in flexible, self-service reporting and dashboard capabilities that let HR leaders pull strategic workforce data without exporting to spreadsheets.</td><td>82% frustration with reporting (Q9), 67% say current tool falls short on reporting (Q17), 32% name it their single biggest frustration (Q18)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2. Integration</td><td>Reduce integration friction between payroll, HRIS, and benefits modules, especially for multi-tool stacks.</td><td>41% frustrated with poor integration (Q9), 39% triggered evaluation due to integration issues (Q7)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3. Right-sizing</td><td>Position the platform as purpose-built for mid-market needs: avoid both under-building and over-building.</td><td>82% rejected competitors for being overbuilt (Q15), 58% identify a mid-market capability gap (Q24)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4. Onboarding UX</td><td>Automate the onboarding workflow, which is a pain point for over a third of participants.</td><td>34% frustrated with manual onboarding (Q9), 28% require onboarding automation as a minimum criterion (Q11)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Suggested Next Steps</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>Share key findings with Product, Marketing, and Sales leadership within one week.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>Prioritize reporting and integration improvements in the next product roadmap cycle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>Develop mid-market positioning messaging that addresses the "overbuilt vs. underbuilt" gap.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>Create competitive battlecards based on rejection reasons (Section 6) for the Sales team.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>Consider a follow-up quantitative study to validate the exploratory segmentation findings with a larger, representative sample.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

  </div>
</details>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 2: TEAM-SPECIFIC RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<details class="accordion">
  <summary class="accordion-header">
    <span class="accordion-num">02</span>
    <span class="accordion-text">
      <span class="accordion-title">Team-Specific Recommendations</span>
      <span class="accordion-subtitle">Strategic and tactical guidance for Marketing, Product, and Sales</span>
    </span>
    <span class="accordion-icon">+</span>
  </summary>
  <div class="accordion-body">

    <!-- MARKETING -->
    <div class="team-section">
      <h3>Marketing Recommendations</h3>

      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Lead with the reporting pain point in top-of-funnel content.</strong> 82% of participants cited reporting inadequacy as a frustration with their current tool (Q9). Content that addresses "why your HR reports fail you" will resonate with this audience at scale.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Position against complexity, not features.</strong> 82% rejected competitors for being overbuilt (Q15), and 58% said the mid-market is underserved by platforms designed for enterprises (Q24). Messaging should emphasize "built for your stage" rather than feature counts.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Highlight the evaluation trigger window.</strong> 69% of evaluations were triggered by manual process burden (Q7) and 48% by growth outpacing system capacity. Target companies showing growth signals (funding announcements, job postings) with timely outreach.</p>
      </div>

      <table class="rec-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Tactic</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Content pillar</td><td>Develop a "Mid-Market HR Tech" content series addressing reporting, integration, and right-sizing. Use the 82%/58% data points as hooks.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Competitive landing pages</td><td>Create comparison pages for ADP (25% of prior tools used), Gusto, and BambooHR. Lead with "overbuilt" and "capability gap" messaging from Q15 rejection data.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lead magnets</td><td>Offer a "HR Tech Evaluation Checklist" based on the minimum criteria data from Q11 (native payroll, multi-state compliance, employee self-service).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- PRODUCT -->
    <div class="team-section">
      <h3>Product Recommendations</h3>

      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Reporting is the single highest-priority gap.</strong> Reporting frustration appears in Q9 (82%), Q17 (67%), Q18 (32%), and Q24 (19%). It spans every company size and every current tool. This is the universal unmet need.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Integration friction drives both evaluation triggers and ongoing frustration.</strong> Poor system integration shows up in Q7 (39%), Q9 (41%), Q17 (41%), and Q18 (24%). Payroll/HRIS integration friction specifically appears in Q7 (28%), Q9 (19%), and Q18 (18%).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Performance management and compensation are emerging gaps.</strong> 38% say their current tool falls short on performance management (Q17), and 32% say compensation needs have emerged since their last evaluation (Q19). These are growth opportunities.</p>
      </div>

      <table class="rec-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Tactic</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Reporting overhaul</td><td>Build self-service dashboards with pre-built templates for headcount, turnover, compensation, and compliance. 20% of participants required reporting capabilities as a minimum criterion (Q11).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Onboarding automation</td><td>34% frustrated with manual onboarding (Q9), 28% required it as table stakes (Q11). Automate document collection, task assignment, and new-hire provisioning.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Multi-state compliance</td><td>41% required multi-state payroll compliance as a minimum criterion (Q11). As remote work drives multi-state employment (28% cited this in Q8), this becomes non-negotiable.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- SALES -->
    <div class="team-section">
      <h3>Sales Recommendations</h3>

      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>The buying committee is cross-functional but HR-led.</strong> 94% of participants were the primary evaluator (Q12), but 91% said the process was cross-functional, and 74% said the CFO held financial approval authority. Sales conversations must address both HR needs and CFO cost concerns.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Demos are a critical conversion point.</strong> 22% cited the demo experience as a reason they chose their current tool (Q14). Structured, personalized demos that mirror the buyer's actual workflows will outperform generic product tours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <p><strong>Switching costs are a real barrier.</strong> 24% cited switching cost as a factor in their decision (Q14), and 43% of Gusto users specifically pointed to switching costs. Address migration pain proactively with implementation support messaging. 28% of participants required implementation support as a minimum criterion (Q11).</p>
      </div>

      <table class="rec-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Tactic</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Discovery questions</td><td>Ask about reporting frustrations (82% prevalence), manual process burden (69%), and growth trajectory. These are the most common triggers and pain points.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Competitive positioning</td><td>Against overbuilt competitors: "You don't need enterprise complexity." Against underbuilt competitors: "You've outgrown basic tools." 76% cited capability gaps in rejected tools (Q15).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pricing strategy</td><td>54% said competitive pricing was a selection factor (Q14). Lead with transparent, per-employee pricing. BambooHR (63%) and Gusto (67%) users are especially price-sensitive.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

  </div>
</details>

